# Responsive UI is required

Every new or changed user-facing UI must be responsive. Treat responsiveness as part of implementation, not follow-up polish.

## Build

- Start mobile-first and let layouts expand progressively.
- Prefer normal flow, Grid/Flexbox, wrapping, `minmax()`, relative units, and existing design-system breakpoints.
- Avoid fixed widths/heights when content or viewport size can vary. Use sensible `min-*` / `max-*` constraints.
- Never require horizontal page scrolling at 320px wide. Only intrinsically wide controls such as data tables may scroll inside a clearly bounded region.
- Keep actions, labels, validation, dialogs, and navigation visible and usable at every supported size.
- Account for long names, translated-length text, browser zoom, safe areas, and mobile viewport height.
- Preserve the clinic's 44px minimum touch-target contract.
- Do not create separate mobile and desktop component trees unless their behavior genuinely differs.

## Verify before completion

- Exercise changed UI at representative phone (320–390px), tablet (~768px), desktop (~1280px), and wide-screen widths.
- Check portrait and landscape where layout or viewport height matters.
- Confirm there is no clipping, overlap, unintended overflow, unreadably narrow content, or functionality available at only one breakpoint.
- Add or update the smallest useful Playwright coverage when responsive behavior is non-trivial or has regressed before; use role/text/test-id selectors per repository rules.

If responsive verification cannot be run, state that explicitly instead of claiming the UI is fully verified.
